    "items/lem-schur-leading-coefficients-and-good-asymptotic-specializations.md": {
      "id": "lem-schur-leading-coefficients-and-good-asymptotic-specializations",
      "deps": [
        "def-finite-weyl-lusztig-basic-set-data",
        "lem-lusztig-asymptotic-algebra-and-specialization-map",
        "thm-hh-canonical-hecke-trace-is-symmetric-and-perfect",
        "def-schur-elements-from-a-symmetrizing-trace",
        "cor-finitely-generated-torsion-free-modules-over-a-pid-are-free",
        "lem-the-laurent-polynomial-ring-is-noetherian-and-a-unique-factorisation-domain"
      ],
      "aliases": [],
      "iota_lines": [
        "4.1 To see positivity, use the rational positive definite form $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\mu(x\\iota(y))$ on $J_{\\mathbb Q}$: [F1] makes its basis $t_w$ orthonormal. Trace symmetry implies $\\langle ax,y\\rangle=\\langle x,\\iota(a)y\\rangle$. A rational simple left ideal $U_E\\subset J_{\\mathbb Q}$ exists from its matrix decomposition and is invariant under both $a$ and $\\iota(a)$; restriction of this positive form to $U_E$ is nondegenerate. Thus their matrices on $U_E$ are adjoints and have equal trace. It follows that $\\chi_E^J(t_{w^{-1}})=\\chi_E^J(t_w)$. Step 2.1 now gives $f_Ed_E=\\sum_w\\chi_E^J(t_w)^2>0$. The strict inequality holds because a nonzero simple character is not identically zero on a split algebra: it takes value one on an appropriate matrix unit. Therefore $f_E>0$. [F1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]"
      ],
      "links_to_scope": [
        "lem-schur-leading-coefficients-and-good-asymptotic-specializations",
        "lem-lusztig-asymptotic-algebra-and-specialization-map"
      ],
      "status": "draft"
    },
    "items/thm-supermodules-equivalent-to-graded-u-modules.md": {
      "id": "thm-supermodules-equivalent-to-graded-u-modules",
      "deps": [
        "def-lie-supermodule",
        "def-universal-enveloping-superalgebra",
        "thm-universal-property-of-the-enveloping-superalgebra"
      ],
      "aliases": [],
      "iota_lines": [
        "Let $\\mathfrak g$ be a complex Lie superalgebra. Restriction along $\\iota:\\mathfrak g\\to U(\\mathfrak g)$ and extension by the universal property give mutually inverse correspondences between left $\\mathfrak g$-supermodules and unital graded left $U(\\mathfrak g)$-modules. The correspondences preserve parity-preserving homomorphisms.",
        "1.2 Conversely, restrict a unital graded left $U(\\mathfrak g)$-module action along $\\iota$. Applying the quotient relation [F2] to $v$ gives $[x,y]v=x(yv)-(-1)^{|x||y|}y(xv)$; grading and bilinearity are inherited from the module action. Thus the restriction is a $\\mathfrak g$-supermodule. [given, F2, algebra]",
        "2.1 Starting from a supermodule, the extension in [F3] restricts to the original action by its defining property. Starting from a $U(\\mathfrak g)$-module, the extension of its restriction agrees with the original action on every generator $\\iota(x)$ and on the unit; these generate the quotient algebra by [F2], so the actions agree on all of $U(\\mathfrak g)$. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]"
      ],
      "links_to_scope": [
        "def-universal-enveloping-superalgebra",
        "thm-universal-property-of-the-enveloping-superalgebra",
        "thm-supermodules-equivalent-to-graded-u-modules"
      ],
      "status": "draft"
    },
