    "items/lem-lusztig-asymptotic-algebra-and-specialization-map.md": {
      "id": "lem-lusztig-asymptotic-algebra-and-specialization-map",
      "deps": [
        "def-finite-weyl-lusztig-basic-set-data"
      ],
      "aliases": [],
      "iota_lines": [
        "Assume [[def-finite-weyl-lusztig-basic-set-data]], including P2\u2013P8 and P15\u2032. The free abelian group $J=\\bigoplus_{w\\in W}\\mathbb Zt_w$ is an associative algebra under $t_xt_y=\\sum_z\\gamma_{x,y,z^{-1}}t_z$, with identity $\\sum_{d\\in\\mathcal D}n_dt_d$. It has an anti-involution $\\iota(t_w)=t_{w^{-1}}$ and a perfect symmetrizing trace $\\mu(t_w)=n_w$ for $w\\in\\mathcal D$ and zero otherwise, with $\\mu(t_xt_y)=\\delta_{x,y^{-1}}$.",
        "3.1 The coefficient of $t_y$ in $t_x\\sum_dn_dt_d$ is $\\sum_dn_d\\gamma_{x,d,y^{-1}}=\\sum_dn_d\\gamma_{y^{-1},x,d}$ by P7. P2 makes it zero unless $y=x$; then P3 and P5 give the unique coefficient $n_d^2=1$. The left identity is proved in the same way, using cyclic symmetry with $\\gamma_{x,y^{-1},d}$; the appropriate unique $d$ comes from P3 applied to $x^{-1}$. Step 1.1 proves that $\\iota$ reverses products. Since $d=d^{-1}$ by P6, $\\mu(t_xt_y)=\\sum_d\\gamma_{x,y,d}n_d$ is $\\delta_{x,y^{-1}}$ by P2, P3 and P5. This symmetric permutation Gram matrix is invertible over $\\mathbb Z$, proving every trace assertion. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]"
      ],
      "links_to_scope": [
        "lem-lusztig-asymptotic-algebra-and-specialization-map"
      ],
      "status": "draft"
    },
